From 035b7bbe6aa65b74319f51134656326d0d8f4429 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 11:06:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] fix(sandbox): copy skills sources without following
 symlinks at any level

A container that can write a skills source (a trusted project's .hermes/skills
under a cwd mounted rw via docker_mount_cwd_to_workspace, or an rw
docker_volumes entry) could swap a vetted file, or any directory above it, for
a symlink to a host file between the walk's check and the copy's open. The
path-based copy (src.stat + shutil.copy2) followed the link and wrote e.g.
HERMES_HOME/.env into the container's own read-only skills mount. The live
refresh re-runs the copy every 5 s on the container's commands, so the race
could be retried forever (review repro: leak after 10-31 refreshes, 3/3 runs).

Problem: race.py leaks TOP-SECRET-HOST-TOKEN into cache/sandbox-skills/<key>/s/zz_payload.md.
Why 1: _copy_if_stale opened the source by path and followed symlinks (copy2).
Why 2: the walk vetted names/paths (is_symlink, deny guard) and the copy re-resolved
       the path later, so check and use referred to different objects (TOCTOU).
Why 3: the copy was written for a host-only, once-per-container source; the
       live-refresh commit made it run repeatedly on a source the sandbox can write.
Root (happened): the copy had no invariant tying "the entry that was vetted" to
       "the bytes that are read"; every source component was re-resolved by path.
Root (not caught): tests only covered static trees (symlinks present before the
       walk); no test changed the tree between enumeration and open.
Recurrence: first time for this copy (parent commit ran it once at creation).

Fix:
- The root is resolved once; the copy key and the walk use that realpath, and it
  is opened component by component from "/" with O_NOFOLLOW|O_DIRECTORY, so an
  ancestor swapped after resolution fails the open and the tree is withheld.
- _walk_skill_tree_anchored walks via directory fds: each subdir is opened
  O_NOFOLLOW relative to its open parent and must be the inode that was listed;
  files are opened O_NOFOLLOW|O_NONBLOCK relative to their dir fd, fstat must
  show S_ISREG, and bytes are copied from that fd (mode & 0o777, ns mtime kept).
  A swapped parent dir therefore can't redirect a file open either.
- Platforms without O_NOFOLLOW/dir_fd opens (Windows) withhold the copy (left
  empty, warned once per root) instead of falling back to a path-following copy.
- iter_skills_files (remote upload backends, whose sandbox can't write the host
  source) still uses the path walk; unchanged.
- Warm no-op refresh of a 5,000-file tree: 121 ms -> 65 ms (cold 648 -> 566 ms).

Proof: tests/tools/test_skills_copy_race.py swaps deterministically from inside
the walk's deny predicate (after vetting, before open): file -> symlink, parent
dir -> symlink after its file was vetted, root ancestor -> symlink, dir ->
symlink before descent, file -> FIFO, plus capability-missing withholding,
symlinked-root portability, and posix/windows platform-lane checks. 4 of these
fail on e38682765006 (file, parent dir, root ancestor, capability) and pass here.
The review's race.py / race_env.py repros: no leak in 200 refreshes (4 runs).
Neighbouring suites (credential_files, docker_environment, docker_skills_mount_live,
file_sync*, ssh_*, capsule, daytona, modal, vercel, skill_usage, ...): 382 passed,
14 skipped. scripts/check: ok.
