+Skills directories are never bind-mounted raw: each root mounts a sanitized copy under `<HERMES_HOME>/cache/sandbox-skills/` (no symlinks, no `skills/.hub` cache, nothing the read deny-list blocks such as a skill's `.env`). Hermes refreshes the copies in place before commands, at most every 5 seconds, so skill edits reach a running container while files created later that the agent may not read on the host never do. The copy reads its source without following symlinks at any level (each file and directory is opened relative to its already-open parent), so a container that can write a skills source, e.g. a project's `.hermes/skills` under a cwd mounted read-write with `docker_mount_cwd_to_workspace`, cannot redirect the copy to other host files by swapping in a symlink. On a platform without those no-follow opens (Windows), the skills copy is left empty rather than copied unsafely.
 
 On startup, Hermes runs `docker ps --filter label=hermes-task-id=<id> --filter label=hermes-profile=<identity>` and **attaches to the existing container** when it finds one. The identity is the active profile unless `docker_shared_container_key` explicitly opts trusted profiles into a common value. If the container is `exited` (e.g. after a Docker daemon restart), it's `docker start`'d and reused — filesystem state and any installed packages survive, but in-container background processes do not.
 
